    {
      "type": "explanation",
      "title": "Nesting: Code Inside Code",
      "content": "You already know if statements and loops. 'Nesting' means putting one inside another. The inner block is indented further — Python uses indentation to know what belongs where. You can put an if inside an if, an if inside a loop, or even a loop inside a loop!",
      "code": "age = 15\nhas_ticket = True\nif age >= 12:\n    if has_ticket:\n        print(\"Enjoy!\")\n    else:\n        print(\"Buy a ticket\")\nelse:\n    print(\"Too young\")",
      "highlights": [
        { "lines": [3], "note": "Outer if — checks the first condition." },
        { "lines": [4, 5, 6, 7], "note": "Inner if/else — only reached if the outer if is True. Notice the extra indentation." },
        { "lines": [8, 9], "note": "Outer else — runs if the outer if is False. The inner if is never checked." }
      ]
    },
